# 1. NEUROANATOMY

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C1 #pC_MK

Part A Reference: Neuroanatomy (Pages 1-10)

Part B Reference: C1 Neuroanesthesia Core Concepts (Pages 1-3)

Part C Reference: Medical Knowledge - Integrated Neuroanatomy

## 1.1. CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM OVERVIEW [PART A]

### 1.1.1. SYLLABUS CORRELATION

* **Part A:** Detailed anatomy of brain and spinal cord `[A:1-10]`.
* **Part B:** Clinical correlations of neuroanatomy to neurosurgical procedures `[B:1-3]`.
* **Part C:** Integration of neuroanatomic knowledge for perioperative management `[C:1]`.

**Exam Weighting:** High (Fundamental to neuroanesthesia)

**Competency Focus:** `[COMP: MK]`, `[COMP: PC]`

**The central nervous system (CNS) is composed of the brain and the spinal cord.**

### 1.1.2. BRAIN STRUCTURE [PART A]

The brain is enclosed by the cranium, a fixed bony cavity, and is physically and functionally divided into two compartments: the supratentorium and the infratentorium `[A:1-2]`.

#### 1.1.2.1. SUPRATENTORIUM

* **Cerebral Hemispheres:** Paired structures divided into four lobes `[A:2]`:
  * Frontal lobe - motor function, personality, intellect
  * Temporal lobe - auditory processing, memory, emotion
  * Parietal lobe - sensory processing, spatial awareness
  * Occipital lobe - visual processing
* **Eloquent Cortical Areas:** Regions responsible for critical functions `[A:2]`:
  * Primary somatosensory and motor cortex - Adjacent to central sulcus in parietal and frontal lobes
  * Language centers (typically left hemisphere) `[A:2]`:
	* Broca area (frontal cortex) - Language formation; lesions cause expressive aphasia
	* Wernicke area (posterior superior temporal cortex) - Language acquisition; lesions cause receptive aphasia
* **Diencephalon:** Located cephalad to midbrain `[A:2]`:
  * Thalamus - Sensory and motor "relay station" connecting cortex with rest of nervous system
  * Hypothalamus - Autonomic and endocrine functions, connected to pituitary gland via infundibulum
* **Limbic System:** Group of structures responsible for cognitive function, memory consolidation, and emotional responses `[A:2-3]`:
  * Components include hippocampus, amygdala, parts of hypothalamus, and some cortical regions (e.g., insular region)

#### 1.1.2.2. EXTRAPYRAMIDAL SYSTEM

* Consists of brain structures that modify motor function but are not components of the corticospinal tract `[A:2]`
* Includes:
  * Basal ganglia (caudate nucleus, globus pallidus, putamen, substantia nigra, red nucleus)
  * Cerebellum
  * Components of auditory and vestibular pathways
* Dysfunction results in difficulty with motor control without frank weakness (e.g., Parkinson disease, essential tremor, ataxia) `[A:2]`

#### 1.1.2.3. INFRATENTORIUM

* **Brainstem:** Comprised of midbrain, pons, and medulla `[A:3]`
  * Responsible for consciousness via reticular activating system
  * Controls autonomic functions including respiratory and cardiovascular control
  * Contains nuclei for cranial nerves III to XII
  * Mediates various reflexes (e.g., cough/gag, pupillary reflexes)
* **Cerebellum:** Located in posterior fossa `[A:3]`
  * Processes proprioceptive input
  * Establishes axial posture and gait
  * Among the most rudimentary parts of the brain

![Gross anatomy of the brain](NEUROLOGY_ANAT_OF_BRAIN.png)

**Bottom Line:** The CNS has a complex anatomical organization with functionally specialized regions. Understanding this neuroanatomy is essential for localizing neurological deficits and planning neurosurgical approaches safely.

### 1.1.3. SPINAL CORD [PART A]

The spinal cord is composed of central gray matter and outer white matter, and is enclosed by the vertebral column `[A:8-9]`.

* **Vertebral Column:** Composed of 33 vertebrae `[A:8]`:
  * 7 cervical
  * 12 thoracic
  * 5 lumbar
  * 9 fused sacral and coccygeal
* **Spinal Cord Composition:** `[A:8-9]`
  * Central gray matter
  * Dorsal columns - Tracts responsible for proprioception and light touch
  * Lateral spinothalamic tracts - Responsible for pain and temperature sensation
  * Outer white matter - Contains lateral corticospinal tracts (motor function)
  * Lateral gray columns - Contain cell bodies of preganglionic sympathetic neurons (T1-L2/L3)
* **Termination:** The spinal cord terminates at L1 or L2 in adults, ending in structures known as the conus medullaris terminus and filum terminale `[A:9]`

![Major ascending and descending spinal cord tracts](NEUROLOGY_NEUROLOGY_NEUROLOGY_nse-6365670355035447390-119673.jpg_885ccf.jpg_2b51bc.jpg_655256.jpg)

#### 1.1.3.1. FUNCTIONAL ORGANIZATION

* **Ascending Tracts:** Carry sensory information to the brain `[A:9]`
  * Dorsal columns - Fine touch, vibration, proprioception
  * Spinothalamic tracts - Pain, temperature
* **Descending Tracts:** Carry motor commands from brain to spinal cord `[A:9]`
  * Corticospinal tracts - Voluntary movement
  * Rubrospinal tracts - Coordination of movement
* **Reflex Arcs:** Pathways for automatic responses to stimuli `[A:9]`
  * Monosynaptic (e.g., stretch reflexes)
  * Polysynaptic (e.g., withdrawal from pain)

**Bottom Line:** The spinal cord has a complex arrangement of ascending and descending pathways that are critical for sensory processing and motor control. The termination of the spinal cord at the L1-L2 level is an important anatomical consideration for lumbar puncture and neuraxial anesthesia.

## 1.2. NEURONAL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTION [PART A]

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C1 #pC_MK

Part A Reference: Neurophysiology (Pages 1-3)

Part B Reference: C1 Neuroanesthesia Core Concepts (Page 2)

Part C Reference: Medical Knowledge - Cellular Neuroanatomy

### 1.2.1. NEURON TYPES AND STRUCTURE [PART A]

**The neuron is the functional unit of the nervous system, coordinating and controlling the other organ systems.** `[A:1]` 

#### 1.2.1.1. FUNCTIONS OF THE NERVOUS SYSTEM

* **Sensory Input:** Sensory receptors detect changes in external and internal environments `[A:1]` 
  * Generate action potentials in response to stimuli
  * Relay information to central nervous system
* **Integration:** Sensory input is processed by CNS and decisions are made `[A:1]` 
* **Motor Output:** Neurons relay action potentials from CNS to muscles and glands `[A:1]` 
  * This is the only way to interact with external environment
  * Even salivation requires smooth muscle contraction

#### 1.2.1.2. NEURON COMPONENTS

Although neurons vary in their detailed cellular structure, all include the following components `[A:1]` 

* **Cell Body:** Contains cytoplasm, nucleus and organelles `[A:1]` 
  * Involved in protein synthesis and ATP generation
  * Mature neurons lack centrioles, preventing mitosis
  * Cell bodies form gray matter in brain and spinal cord
  * Groupings of cell bodies are termed nuclei in CNS and ganglia in PNS
* **Dendrites:** Branched projections that receive signals from other neurons `[A:1]` 
  * Propagate signals toward the cell body
* **Axon:** Long projection originating at cell body `[A:1]` 
  * Action potentials generated at axon hillock and conducted away from cell body
  * May be myelinated or unmyelinated
  * Myelin consists of multiple layers of electrically insulating lipid and protein
  * Produced by Schwann cells in PNS or oligodendrocytes in CNS
  * Lipid gives axons white color (white matter)
  * Increases speed of action potential propagation
* **Axon Terminal:** Distal end of axon `[A:1]` 
  * Communicates with another cell through a synapse
  * In motor neurons, this is the neuromuscular junction

![Basic structure of a neuron](NEUROLOGY_Basic_Structure_of_a_Neuron.jpg)

#### 1.2.1.3. NEURON MORPHOLOGIES

These components can be arranged to give differing nerve morphologies `[A:2]` 

* **Unipolar:** Axon projecting from cell body
  * Not common in humans but found in cochlear
* **Bipolar:** Cell body between dendrites and axon
  * Found in retina and olfactory neurons
* **Pseudounipolar:** Appears unipolar with cell body interrupting axon midway
  * Most sensory neurons are pseudounipolar
* **Multipolar:** Dendrites insert directly into cell body without coalescing
  * Classical example is motor neuron
* **Anaxonic:** Dendrites and axons indistinguishable
  * Example: amacrine cells in retina
* **Pyramidal Cells:** Triangular cell body with single axon and many dendrites
  * Can integrate many afferent signals
  * Commonly found in cerebral cortex and hippocampus

![Common types of neuron](NEUROLOGY_Type_of_Neurons.jpeg)

**Bottom Line:** Neurons are the functional units of the nervous system with specialized structures that facilitate their roles in receiving, integrating, and transmitting information. Their diverse morphologies reflect specialized functions in different parts of the nervous system.

### 1.2.2. NERVOUS SYSTEM ORGANIZATION [PART A]

The nervous system works holistically but is traditionally divided into several parts `[A:3]` 

#### 1.2.2.1. CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (CNS)

* **Brain:** Site of higher integration `[A:3]` 
  * Processes sensory inputs and motor outputs
  * Controls thinking and learning
* **Spinal Cord:** Contains long sensory and motor pathways `[A:3]` 
  * Conveys information between periphery and CNS
  * Contains local integrative networks and interneurons

#### 1.2.2.2. PERIPHERAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (PNS)

* **Sensory (Afferent) Neurons:** `[A:3]` 
  * Relay information from external environment and viscera to CNS
  * Most have pseudounipolar structure with cell body in dorsal root ganglion
* **Motor (Efferent) Neurons:** `[A:3]` 
  * Under voluntary control
  * Transmit action potentials from CNS to skeletal muscle
  * Typically multipolar, receiving multiple inputs through dendrites
* **Enteric Neurons:** `[A:3]` 
  * Form network around gastrointestinal tract
  * Sometimes referred to as "second brain"
  * Operates with relative independence from CNS
* **Autonomic Neurons:** `[A:3]` 
  * Deal with "housekeeping" functions of viscera and emergency responses
  * Involuntary system divided into:
	* Sympathetic nervous system
	* Parasympathetic nervous system

#### 1.2.2.3. COMMUNICATION METHODS

* **Neural Signaling:** `[A:1]` 
  * Action potential conducted from cell body to terminus
  * Transmission between cells via chemical or electrical synapse
  * Relatively rapid (up to 120 m/s in large myelinated nerves)
* **Endocrine Signaling:** `[A:1]` 
  * Brain synthesizes and releases hormones into circulation
  * Much slower than neural signaling

**Bottom Line:** The nervous system is organized into central and peripheral components that work together to process sensory input, make decisions, and produce motor output. This organization allows for both rapid neural responses and slower endocrine regulation.

### 1.2.3. PERIPHERAL NERVE STRUCTURE [PART A]

A peripheral nerve is a bundle of many nerve axons with a layered structure `[A:3]` 

* **Schwann Cells:** Produce myelin sheath around axons in PNS `[A:3]` 
  * Axon with myelin is called a nerve fiber
* **Endoneurium:** Thin connective tissue layer surrounding each nerve fiber `[A:3]` 
* **Perineurium:** Wraps around bundles of nerve fibers (fascicles) `[A:3]` 
  * Provides structural support
* **Epineurium:** Thick outer layer of peripheral nerve `[A:3]` 
  * Encloses multiple fascicles and their blood vessels

💡 **Clinical Application:** For local anesthetic to exert effects on nerve axons, it must diffuse through three layers: epineurium, perineurium, and endoneurium `[A:3]` .

**Bottom Line:** Peripheral nerves have a complex layered structure that provides physical protection and electrical insulation for axons. Understanding this anatomy is important for techniques like regional anesthesia and managing peripheral nerve injuries.

## 1.3. CEREBROVASCULAR SYSTEM [PART A]

### 1.3.1. SYLLABUS CORRELATION

* **Part A:** Cerebral and spinal blood supply `[A:3-7]`, `[A:9-10]`.
* **Part B:** Clinical implications of vascular anatomy for neurosurgical procedures `[B:1-2]`.
* **Part C:** Integrating vascular knowledge for perioperative management of neurovascular cases `[C:1]`.

**Exam Weighting:** High (Critical for understanding cerebral perfusion)

**Competency Focus:** `[COMP: MK]`, `[COMP: PC]`

### 1.3.2. CEREBRAL BLOOD SUPPLY [PART A]

**The brain receives approximately 70% of its blood supply from two internal carotid arteries anteriorly and 30% from two vertebral arteries posteriorly.** `[A:3]`

#### 1.3.2.1. ANTERIOR CIRCULATION

* **Common Carotid Artery:** Originates from the aortic arch and divides at the level of the thyroid cartilage into internal and external carotid arteries `[A:3-4]`
* **Internal Carotid Artery (ICA):** `[A:4]`
  * Traverses the skull base through the foramen lacerum
  * Travels through the cavernous sinus and into the carotid groove
  * Divides into:
	* Posterior communicating artery (PCOM)
	* Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)
	* Middle cerebral artery (MCA)

#### 1.3.2.2. POSTERIOR CIRCULATION

* **Vertebral Arteries:** Originate from the subclavian arteries and converge to form the basilar artery at the pontomedullary junction `[A:7]`
* **Basilar Artery:** `[A:7]`
  * Runs along the ventral surface of the brainstem
  * At the midbrain level, divides into:
	* Posterior cerebral arteries (PCAs)
	* Anastomoses with PCOMs to complete the circle of Willis

#### 1.3.2.3. CIRCLE OF WILLIS

* An anastomotic ring at the base of the skull `[A:3]`
* Less than 50% of people have a "complete" circle of Willis `[A:7]` `[A:9]` 
* Provides collateral circulation to maintain brain perfusion if one vessel is occluded
* Components include `[A:4-5]` 
  * Anterior cerebral arteries (ACA) - Supply superior and medial portions of cerebral hemispheres
  * Anterior communicating artery (ACom) - Connects left and right ACAs
  * Middle cerebral arteries (MCA) - Supply lateral aspect of cerebral hemispheres
  * Posterior cerebral arteries (PCA) - Supply occipital lobes and medial temporal lobes
  * Posterior communicating arteries (PCom) - Connect PCAs to ICAs

![The circle of Willis](NEUROLOGY_Circle_of_Willis.png)

#### 1.3.2.4. CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE: STROKE

The Bamford classification divides ischemic strokes into categories based on affected cerebral circulation `[A:5]` 

* **Anterior Circulation Infarct (ACPI):**
  * Thrombus or embolus in anterior circulation (MCA most commonly)
  * Causes:
	* Contralateral weakness
	* Homonymous hemianopia
	* Higher cerebral dysfunction (dysphasia or visuospatial disorder)
* **Posterior Circulation Infarct:**
  * Affects vertebrobasilar circulation
  * Symptoms include:
	* Cerebellar dysfunction (ataxia, nystagmus)
	* Cranial nerve palsies
	* Loss of consciousness from brainstem involvement
	* Homonymous hemianopia from occipital lobe involvement
* **Lacunar Infarct:**
  * Occlusion of small subcortical vessel
  * Clinical effects (e.g., motor hemiparesis) disproportionate to small infarct size
  * Affects deep structures like thalamus or internal capsule
  * Better prognosis than larger strokes

💡 While the circle of Willis should theoretically provide collateral circulation when a vessel is occluded, it is often anatomically incomplete, contributing to stroke remaining the third leading cause of death in the UK `[A:9]` .

### 1.3.3. VENOUS DRAINAGE [PART A]

**Cerebral Venous System:** Consists of both deep and superficial veins that ultimately drain into the dural venous sinuses `[A:7]`

* **Superficial System:** `[A:7]` `[A:7]` 
  * Dural venous sinuses - Venous channels located between two layers of dura mater
  * Blood from dural sinuses drains into paired internal jugular veins
  * Unique features compared to other veins:
	* Formed from dura, lack valves
	* Not collapsible
	* Higher risk of air embolus during neurosurgery
* **Deep System:** `[A:7]` `[A:7]` 
  * Traditional veins draining blood from deep brain structures
  * Merge to form vein of Galen
  * Drains into inferior sagittal sinus

![Gross anatomy of the venous drainage system of the brain](NEUROLOGY_Venous_Drainage_of_Brain.png)

### 1.3.4. SPINAL CORD BLOOD SUPPLY [PART A]

**Arterial Supply:** The spinal cord receives its blood supply from one anterior spinal artery and two posterior spinal arteries `[A:9]`

* **Anterior Spinal Artery:** `[A:9]`
  * Originates from six to eight major radicular arteries derived from the aorta
  * Supplies the anterior two-thirds of the spinal cord
  * The artery of Adamkiewicz (usually at T11 or T12) is the largest radicular artery and generally supplies T8 to the conus medullaris terminus
* **Posterior Spinal Arteries:** `[A:9]`
  * Originate from the posterior inferior cerebellar arteries
  * Supply the dorsal horns and white matter (posterior third of the spinal cord)

**Venous Drainage:** `[A:9]`
* Three anterior and three posterior spinal veins located in the pia mater
* Anastomose to form a tortuous venous plexus
* Blood from this plexus drains into the epidural venous plexus

![The spinal cord blood supply](NEUROLOGY_Spinal_Cord_Blood_Supply.jpg)

#### 1.3.4.1. CLINICAL RELEVANCE: ANTERIOR SPINAL ARTERY SYNDROME

The artery of Adamkiewicz most commonly arises from the left posterior intercostal artery (branch of aorta). Damage or obstruction can occur through atherosclerotic disease, aortic dissection or surgical clamping during aortic aneurysm repair `[A:9-10]`.

Signs and symptoms include:

* Paraplegia (lower motor neuron deficit at level of lesion, upper motor neuron deficit below)
* Loss of pain and temperature sensation (damage to spinothalamic tracts)
* Autonomic dysfunction involving bladder or bowel
* Preservation of proprioception and vibration sensation (dorsal columns supplied by posterior spinal arteries)

**Bottom Line:** Understanding cerebrovascular anatomy is crucial for neurosurgical procedures and managing patients with cerebrovascular disease. The circle of Willis provides potential collateral circulation, though it is incomplete in many individuals. The artery of Adamkiewicz is a critical vessel for thoracolumbar spinal cord perfusion.

## 1.4. CEREBROSPINAL FLUID SYSTEM [PART A/B]

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C1 #pC_MK

Part A Reference: Neurophysiology (Pages 7-8)

Part B Reference: C1 Neuroanesthesia Core Concepts (Page 2)

Part C Reference: Medical Knowledge - CSF Physiology and Management

### 1.4.1. SYLLABUS CORRELATION

* **Part A:** CSF production, circulation, and reabsorption `[A:7-8]`.
* **Part B:** Clinical implications of CSF physiology for ICP management `[B:2]`.
* **Part C:** Integration of CSF knowledge for perioperative management `[C:1]`.

**Exam Weighting:** Medium

**Competency Focus:** `[COMP: MK]`, `[COMP: PC]`

### 1.4.2. PRODUCTION AND CIRCULATION [PART A]

**Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is a specialist extracellular ultrafiltrate of plasma surrounding the central nervous system.** `[A:7]`

* **Volume and Production:** `[A:7]` `[A:10]` 
  * Total volume in an adult: approximately 150 mL
  * Production rate: 15-20 mL/h (300-500 mL/day)
  * Primarily produced by the choroid plexus of the lateral and third ventricles
  * Production depends on cerebral perfusion pressure - if CPP drops below 70 mmHg, CSF production decreases `[B:2]`
* **Circulation Pathway:** `[A:7]` `[A:10]` 
  * From lateral ventricles via foramina of Monro to third ventricle
  * Through aqueduct of Sylvius to fourth ventricle
  * Exit fourth ventricle via:
	* Foramen of Magendie (central)
	* Lateral foramina of Luschka
  * Flow to subarachnoid space in the cranium via interconnected cisterns at the base of the brain
  * Some CSF traverses the foramen magnum, entering the subarachnoid space within the spinal column
* **Reabsorption:** `[A:7]` `[A:11]` 
  * Primarily into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus, via arachnoid villi and granulations (90%)
  * Small amount absorbed directly into spinal veins (10%)
  * Reabsorption is determined in part by the difference between CSF pressure and venous pressure

![Cerebrospinal fluid compartments](NEUROLOGY_CSF_Fluids_Compartments.png)

### 1.4.3. COMPOSITION AND FUNCTION [PART A/B]

**Functions of CSF:** `[B:2]`
* Regulation of intracranial pressure (part of compensatory mechanisms)
* Mechanical protection of the brain
  * Acts as shock absorber
  * Buoyancy reduces effective brain weight from 1.5 kg to 50 g, reducing brain inertia and protecting against deformation
* Maintenance of stable chemical environment
* Control of respiration via central chemoreceptors

**Composition Compared to Plasma:** `[B:2]` `[A:11]` 

| mmol/litre | Plasma | CSF |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sodium | 145 | 140 |
| Calcium | 2.0 | 1.2 |
| Potassium | 4.0-4.5 | 3 |
| Glucose | 6 | 4 |
| Chloride | 100 | **120** (higher in CSF) |
| Bicarbonate | 22-28 | 22-28 |
| Osmolality | 280-300 | 290 |
| PaCO2 | 5.3 | 6.6 |
| pH | 7.35-7.45 | 7.28-7.32 |
| Protein | 70 g/l | 0.2-0.4 g/l |
| White cell count | 4000-5000/mm³ | 0-5/mm³ |

### 1.4.4. CLINICAL APPLICATIONS AND ANALYSIS [PART B]

**CSF Circulation Disorders:** `[B:2]` `[A:11]` 
* **Production Issues:**
  * Excessive production (e.g., choroid plexus tumors)
  * Reduced production (e.g., poor brain perfusion)
* **Obstructive Issues:**
  * Tumors
  * Bleeding
  * Abscesses
  * Meningeal disease
  * Congenital abnormalities
* **Reabsorption Problems:**
  * Infection
  * Hemorrhage
  * Increased venous pressure

**Diagnostic Analysis of CSF:** `[B:2]`
* **Parameters Assessed:**
  * Pressure
  * Visual appearance
  * Glucose level (normally 66% of fasting plasma glucose)
	* <33% suggests bacterial infection, fungal infection, or malignancy
	* Elevated in viral meningitis
  * Lactate (high in bacterial/fungal meningitis)
  * Cell counts (RBC, WBC with differential)
  * Culture and staining
  * Protein content (elevated in various conditions including bacterial meningitis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, tumors)

**Hydrocephalus:** `[A:11]` 
* Abnormal resistance to CSF circulation or impaired CSF absorption
* Production rate exceeds circulation/absorption rate, resulting in increased ventricular CSF pressure
* Leads to ventriculomegaly and potential brain damage from compression
* **Classification by Obstruction Site:**
  * No obstruction - Usually from overproduction (rare, e.g., choroid plexus papilloma)
  * Foramina of Monro - Blocked by tumor compression
  * Aqueduct of Sylvius - Congenital stenosis, subarachnoid hemorrhage, tumor compression
  * Fourth ventricle outlets - Obstruction from hemorrhage, meningitis, Arnold-Chiari malformation
  * Arachnoid granulations - Absorption obstruction from blood clots
* **Management:**
  * External ventricular drain (EVD) - Temporary CSF drainage, also allows sampling and medication administration
  * Ventricular shunt - Diverts CSF to peritoneal cavity or right atrium
  * Endoscopic third ventriculostomy - Creates opening in floor of third ventricle to bypass obstructions

**Bottom Line:** CSF provides critical mechanical and physiological support to the CNS. Understanding CSF dynamics is essential for managing intracranial pressure and interpreting CSF analysis in various neurological conditions.

## 1.5. BLOOD-BRAIN BARRIER [PART A]

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C1 #pC_MK

Part A Reference: Neurophysiology (Pages 12-13)

Part B Reference: C1 Neuroanesthesia Core Concepts (Page 3)

Part C Reference: Medical Knowledge - Neuropharmacology

### 1.5.1. FUNCTIONS [PART A]

**The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is a physiological, cellular and metabolic barrier at the level of the cerebral capillaries that restricts the free movement of substances between capillaries and brain extracellular fluid.** `[A:12]` 

The BBB has several key functions:

* **Maintain Constant Extracellular Environment** `[A:12]` 
  * Most important function
  * Concentration of solutes in blood varies considerably
  * Neuronal function may be adversely affected by direct exposure to these variations
  * Example: Exercise produces changes in plasma pH and K+ concentration that could depress neuronal activity if transmitted to CNS
* **Protect Brain from Harmful Substances** `[A:12]` 
  * Prevents neurotoxic blood-borne compounds from reaching brain tissue
* **Prevent Release of CNS Neurotransmitters** `[A:12]` 
  * Maintains separation between peripheral circulation and CNS neurochemistry

### 1.5.2. ANATOMICAL LAYERS [PART A]

The BBB comprises three layers `[A:12]` 

* **Capillary Endothelial Cells**
  * Interconnected by tight junctions
  * Restricts passage of substances from capillaries to brain ECF
  * Higher density of mitochondria than extracerebral capillaries
  * Forms a virtually impenetrable barrier to lipophobic molecules
* **Thick Basement Membrane**
  * Lies beneath endothelial cells
* **Astrocyte Foot Processes**
  * Projections from astrocyte glial cells that encircle and are closely applied to capillaries
  * Secrete chemicals that reduce permeability of capillary endothelial cells
  * Choroid plexus lacks this astrocyte coverage, making it more permeable

### 1.5.3. SUBSTANCE TRANSPORT [PART A]

Molecules cross the BBB through several mechanisms `[A:12-13]` 

* **Simple Diffusion**
  * Small, lipid-soluble molecules cross phospholipid bilayers easily
  * Examples: O₂, CO₂, ethanol, steroid hormones
* **Active Transport**
  * Controls passage of small ions (Na⁺, K⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻, H⁺)
  * High density of mitochondria reflects metabolic demands of this process
  * Allows blood and CSF to differ in ion concentrations
* **Facilitated Diffusion**
  * Transport along concentration gradients
  * Glucose transported by GLUT1 transporters (non-ATP dependent)
  * Water transported through aquaporin channels
* **Pinocytosis**
  * Some molecules (e.g., insulin) cross via this vesicular transport mechanism

**Important Exclusions:**
* **Catecholamines** (noradrenaline, dopamine) - Act as neurotransmitters in CNS
  * Note: L-DOPA (dopamine precursor) can cross via facilitated diffusion, making it useful in Parkinson's disease treatment
* **Amino Acids** - Tightly regulated transport, some act as neurotransmitters
* **Ammonia** - Small lipophilic molecule rapidly metabolized to glutamine in astrocytes

### 1.5.4. CLINICAL APPLICATIONS [PART A/B]

#### 1.5.4.1. DRUG TRANSPORT ACROSS THE BBB

The BBB presents a major obstacle to CNS drug delivery. Drugs may cross the BBB through various mechanisms `[A:13]` 

* **Simple Diffusion of Lipid-Soluble Drugs**
  * Examples: propofol, thiopentone, volatile anesthetics, benzodiazepines, phenytoin
* **Direct CNS Administration (Bypassing BBB)**
  * Intrathecal methotrexate
  * Intracerebral implantation of polymer-bound chemotherapy
* **Transmembrane Carriers**
  * L-DOPA transported via neutral amino acid transporter
* **Increased Lipophilicity**
  * Diamorphine crosses BBB 100 times more easily than morphine due to greater lipophilicity
* **BBB Weakening**
  * Inflammation allows antibiotics to cross during meningitis

#### 1.5.4.2. CNS STRUCTURES OUTSIDE THE BBB

Certain structures require direct connection with systemic circulation while remaining protected `[A:13]` 

* **Circumventricular Organs** - Located around third ventricle with "leaky" BBB (fenestrated capillaries):
  * Area postrema (chemoreceptor trigger zone) - Detects noxious substances to trigger vomiting
  * Hypothalamus - Contains osmoreceptors monitoring blood osmolarity
  * Pituitary gland - Secretes eight hormones directly into circulation
  * Pineal gland - Secretes melatonin
  * Choroid plexus - Uses plasma to produce CSF

⚠️ While BBB is deficient in circumventricular organs, the blood-CSF barrier remains intact, preventing substances from entering CSF and accessing brain neurons.

**Bottom Line:** The blood-brain barrier is a critical protective interface controlling molecular access to the brain parenchyma. Understanding its structure and transport mechanisms is essential for CNS drug delivery and explaining the central effects of various medications and toxins. This knowledge has important implications for neuroanesthesia and neuropharmacology.

## 1.6. AUTONOMIC NERVOUS SYSTEM [PART A]

Tags: #pA_physio #pB_C1 #pC_MK

Part A Reference: Neuroanatomy (Page 10)

Part B Reference: C1 Neuroanesthesia Core Concepts (Page 3)

Part C Reference: Medical Knowledge - ANS Pharmacology

### 1.6.1. SYLLABUS CORRELATION

* **Part A:** Detailed anatomy and physiology of autonomic nervous system `[A:10]`.
* **Part B:** Clinical implications for perioperative autonomic responses `[B:3]`.
* **Part C:** Pharmacological management of autonomic responses `[C:1]`.

**Exam Weighting:** Medium

**Competency Focus:** `[COMP: MK]`, `[COMP: PC]`

### 1.6.2. ORGANIZATION AND STRUCTURE [PART A]

**The autonomic nervous system is the involuntary, automatic branch of the nervous system that is largely concerned with control and maintenance of homeostasis.**

* **General Organization:** `[A:10]`
  * Both sympathetic and parasympathetic pathways have two nerves between the central nervous system and target organs
  * Both have a myelinated preganglionic nerve, a ganglion, and then a non-myelinated postganglionic nerve reaching the target organ
  * Both systems exert effects on most organs, typically with one system having dominant resting tone

![Organization of the nervous system](NEUROLOGY_NEUROLOGY_NEUROLOGY_nse-7539566411978546481-120704.jpg_52b45a.jpg_56f44d.jpg_d68d1a.jpg)

### 1.6.3. SYMPATHETIC SYSTEM [PART A]

**The sympathetic nervous system is associated with the 'fight or flight' response.**

* **Outflow:** Thoracolumbar (T1-L2) via anterior or ventral nerve roots (white rami) `[A:10]`
* **Ganglia Location:** `[A:10]`
  * Ganglia further from target organ, in sympathetic chain
  * Short preganglionic fibers travel through white rami communicantes to sympathetic chain and three main plexi
  * Sympathetic chain components:
	* 3 cervical ganglia (superior, middle, lower)
	  * The lower cervical ganglion often fuses with T1 ganglion to form the **stellate ganglion**
	* 12 thoracic ganglia
	* 4 lumbar ganglia
	* 4 sacral ganglia
	* Major plexi: cardiac, coeliac, hypogastric
* **Stellate Ganglion:** `[A:10]`
  * Formed from fusion of first thoracic and inferior cervical ganglion
  * Found at the level of C7, anterior to the neck of the first rib
  * Located medial to vertebral artery, behind the carotid sheath
  * Clinical relevance: Local anesthetic tracking to the stellate ganglion can produce Horner's syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis)
* **Organs with Exclusively Sympathetic Innervation:** `[A:10]`
  * Sweat glands
  * Piloerector muscles
  * Adrenal medulla
  * Juxtaglomerular apparatus
  * Fat tissue

### 1.6.4. PARASYMPATHETIC SYSTEM [PART A]

**The parasympathetic system is associated with 'rest and digest' functions, generally in opposition to the sympathetic system.**

* **Outflow:** Craniosacral `[A:10]`
  * **Cranial:** Via cranial nerves 3, 7, 9, and 10
	* CN III (Oculomotor) - Ciliary ganglion to pupil
	* CN VII (Facial) - Pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia to salivary and lacrimal glands
	* CN IX (Glossopharyngeal) - Otic ganglion to respiratory tree
	* CN X (Vagus) - To heart and GI tract (75% of parasympathetic fibers)
  * **Sacral:** S2-S4 supplying pelvic organs
* **Ganglia Location:** `[A:10]`
  * Ganglia close to target organs
  * Long preganglionic and short postganglionic fibers
* **Organs with Exclusively Parasympathetic Innervation:** `[A:10]`
  * Lacrimal glands

### 1.6.5. NEUROTRANSMITTERS AND RECEPTORS [PART A]

**Neurotransmitters in the Autonomic Nervous System:** `[A:10]`

* **Parasympathetic:**
  * Pre-ganglionic: Acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors
  * Post-ganglionic: Acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors
* **Sympathetic:**
  * Pre-ganglionic: Acetylcholine at nicotinic receptors
  * Post-ganglionic: Noradrenaline at adrenergic receptors
	* Exception: Sweat glands use acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors
  * The adrenal medulla is unique - pre-synaptic neuron synapses directly onto chromaffin cells with acetylcholine as neurotransmitter

**Muscarinic Receptor Subtypes:** `[A:10]`
* M1: Pupil constriction, gastric acid secretion
* M2: Heart
* M3: Vascular, salivary, lacrimal, pancreatic
* M4: Brain, adrenal medulla
* M5: Brain

**Functional Effects by Organ System:** `[A:10]`

| Organ system | Sympathetic | Parasympathetic |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Visual | Pupillary dilation | Pupillary constriction, lacrimation |
| Thermoregulatory | Piloerection, sweating in hands | General sweating |
| Respiratory | Hyperventilation, bronchodilation, reduced secretion volume | Bronchoconstriction, increased secretions |
| Cardiovascular | Vasodilation to skeletal muscle, vasoconstriction in peripheries and splanchnic circulation, stimulates release of adrenaline and noradrenaline from adrenals | Vasodilation to splanchnic circulation |
| Digestive Tract | Decreased secretions, reduced motility, constriction of sphincters, inhibition of gallbladder contraction | Increased motility and secretions, salivation and sphincter relaxation, stimulation of gallbladder |
| Reproductive | Ejaculation | Erection |
| Urinary | Bladder relaxation | Bladder contraction, urinary sphincter relaxation |

**Bottom Line:** The autonomic nervous system consists of sympathetic ("fight or flight") and parasympathetic ("rest and digest") divisions that typically work in opposition. Understanding the anatomical organization, neurotransmitters, and receptor types is crucial for predicting effects of autonomic drugs and managing autonomic responses during anesthesia.

